<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- Curriculum sub-strand: Core 4.2 -->

<chapter xml:id="ch-integration" component="core" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Integration</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      In differentiation you start with a function and find its gradient. Integration is the reverse process: you start with the gradient function and work back to the function it came from.
    </p>
    <p>
      In this chapter you will see how integration reverses differentiation, and find indefinite and definite integrals of polynomials. You will use integration to find the exact area under a curve, compare it with the estimates given by the trapezoidal and mid-ordinate rules, and work out the errors in those estimates. Finally you will apply integration to kinematics, finding velocity from acceleration and displacement from velocity.
    </p>
    <p>
      Integration is used to find areas, volumes and distances exactly. Engineers use it to work out the capacity of a dam, and scientists use it to describe motion.
    </p>
  </introduction>

